course: euphemisms
lesson: 5
title: Soft words for hard things: illness, disability and the body
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some illnesses used to be a secret or a shame, so people invented soft words to talk about them quietly: drinking too much, illness of the mind, and growths that might be cancer.
explain: Old-fashioned phrases hid a lot: "nervous exhaustion" meant a breakdown, and "went away for a rest" could mean months in a mental hospital.
explain: Hospitals still use calm words: "in a comfortable condition" can describe a patient who is badly hurt but steady, not one who feels cosy.
explain: Small, embarrassing things about the body get soft or fancy names too: "body odour" for sweaty smell, "halitosis" for bad breath, "unmentionables" for underwear.
explain: Vivid example: "Uncle Bill is rather fond of a drink" sounds sweet, but it usually means Uncle Bill drinks too much alcohol.
fact: "Fond of a drink" means drinks too much alcohol; it is old-fashioned and British.
fact: "Self-medicating" means using alcohol or drugs to cope with problems instead of getting proper help.
fact: "In recovery" means recovering from an addiction, such as to alcohol or drugs, and staying off it.
fact: "Lost his marbles" means became mentally confused or went mad; it is humorous and can be unkind.
fact: "Confused", said of an old person by carers, often means suffering from dementia.
fact: "Memory problems" is a polite way of saying dementia, an illness of the brain that makes people forget.
fact: "Learning difficulties" is the British official term for an intellectual disability.
fact: "On the spectrum" means autistic: the person's brain works differently, so things like chatting, noise or changes of plan can feel hard.
fact: "Tummy bug" means a stomach infection with being sick or diarrhoea; it is everyday British English.
fact: "Under the knife" means having surgery.
fact: "Laid up" means ill or injured and stuck in bed.
fact: "A growth" means a tumour, a lump that may or may not be cancer.
fact: "A shadow on the lung" means a mark on an X-ray that may be a tumour in the lung.
fact: "In a comfortable condition" in a hospital statement means seriously ill but stable.
fact: "A touch of the sun" means mild sunstroke (feeling ill from too much hot sun); it is old-fashioned and British.
fact: "Nervous exhaustion" is an old-fashioned phrase for a mental breakdown.
fact: "Went away for a rest" is an old-fashioned phrase for spending time in a psychiatric hospital, a hospital for illnesses of the mind.
fact: "Unmentionables" is a humorous word for underwear.
fact: "Body odour" (BO) is the polite name for the smell of sweat.
fact: "Halitosis" is the medical-sounding name for bad breath.
exercise: "Uncle Bill is fond of a drink." What does that usually mean? | He drinks too much alcohol | He loves fruit juice and fizzy pop | He collects old bottles | He works in a cafe
exercise: A doctor says "he's been self-medicating with alcohol". What does it mean? | He drinks to cope with his problems | He takes all his pills correctly on time | He makes his own medicine | He is a doctor himself
exercise: "My aunt has been in recovery for five years." What does it mean? | She has stayed off an addiction for five years | She has been in hospital for five years after a fall | She broke her leg five years ago | She has been on holiday for five years
exercise: "The old professor has lost his marbles." What does it mean? | He has become mentally confused | He has lost his old box of toy marbles | He has lost his job | He has lost a game
exercise: A care home nurse says "your grandmother gets a bit confused." What does it often mean? | She has dementia | She gets lost in big shops | She is bad at maths | She mixes up her shoes
exercise: "Grandpa has memory problems now." What illness is this often? | Dementia | A cold | A broken arm | Bad eyesight
exercise: A British school report says "a pupil with learning difficulties". What does it mean? | A pupil with an intellectual disability | A pupil who is lazy | A pupil who has missed a lot of school this year | A pupil learning a new language
exercise: "My cousin is on the spectrum." What does it mean? | My cousin is autistic | My cousin studies rainbows | My cousin is colour-blind | My cousin is on a TV show
exercise: "I can't come, I've got a tummy bug." What is wrong? | A stomach infection | An insect in the stomach | A sore tummy from laughing | A full tummy after lunch
exercise: "Dad goes under the knife next week." What will happen to Dad? | He will have surgery | He will be attacked | He will cut his hair | He will start cooking
exercise: "Gran's laid up with a bad back." What does it mean? | She is stuck in bed | She is lying in the garden | She is doing exercises | She is laying the table
exercise: The doctor found "a growth" on his neck. What is it? | A lump or tumour | A new grey hair | A big muscle | A small tattoo
exercise: "The X-ray showed a shadow on the lung." What does it mean? | There may be a tumour in the lung | The X-ray was taken in the dark | Someone stood in front of the X-ray | The lungs are very healthy
exercise: A hospital says "the driver is in a comfortable condition." What does it mean? | He is seriously ill but stable | He is sitting in a soft chair | He is feeling very happy | He is ready to go home
exercise: An old British story says "he had a touch of the sun". What does it mean? | He had mild sunstroke | He got a nice suntan | He touched a hot stove | He saw the sunrise
exercise: An old newspaper: "The minister resigned with nervous exhaustion." What does it mean? | He had a mental breakdown | He was a little sleepy | He was nervous before a speech | He was tired of his job
exercise: In an old novel, "she went away for a rest" after a hard year. What does it often mean? | She stayed in a psychiatric hospital | She went to the beach for a sunny holiday | She took a nap | She visited a friend
exercise: A shop sign says "Ladies' unmentionables, second floor." What is sold there? | Underwear | Secret gifts | Cleaning products | Medicine
exercise: An advert says "Stops body odour all day!" What does it stop? | The smell of sweat | Spots on the skin | Feeling tired | Sunburn
exercise: A toothpaste advert talks about "halitosis". What is it? | Bad breath | A tooth falling out | A sore throat | Bleeding gums
test: News: "Three people were hurt. One is in a comfortable condition." What is true of that person? | Seriously hurt but stable | Not hurt at all | Already at home | Sitting in a soft bed reading
test: Chatty: "ugh got a tummy bug, been up all night, not coming in" Why is the speaker staying home? | They have a stomach infection | A bug bit them | They ate too much dinner | They have homework
test: Max says "my grandad's lost his marbles, he forgot my name again". What does Max mean? | His grandad is becoming confused and forgetful | His grandad lost a bag of marbles in the park | His grandad is playing a joke | His grandad lost a game
test: A care home note: "Mr Wood is sometimes confused at night." What would a nurse probably do? | Watch over him because of his dementia | Teach him maths | Give him a puzzle to keep him busy at night | Send him home
test: Gossip: "His wife? Went away for a rest after the baby, if you know what I mean." What is the speaker hinting at? | She spent time in a mental hospital | She went on a nice holiday by the seaside | She slept a lot | She visited her mother
test: A friend whispers "Ollie's got BO, no one wants to sit next to him." What is the problem? | Ollie smells of sweat | Ollie is very loud | Ollie is a bad sport | Ollie has a cold
test: Dad: "Bloody hell, Gran's been under the knife again." What happened to Gran? | She had another operation | She cut herself cooking | She was attacked | She had her hair cut
test: A doctor says "we've found a growth, but it's probably harmless". What is true? | There is a lump that is probably not cancer | Someone has grown taller | A plant is growing in the room | The patient is perfectly healthy everywhere
test: Lily says "Uncle Joe's in recovery, so is he still in hospital?" What should she be told? | Not always; it means he is staying off an addiction | Yes, he is in a recovery room after an operation | No, it means he found something lost | Yes, it means he broke a bone
test: Gran says "Don't sit in the garden all day, you'll get a touch of the sun." What is she warning about? | Mild sunstroke | A nice suntan | Being touched by a bee | Getting too cold
test: An old letter: "Father is laid up with gout." Where is Father? | Stuck in bed, ill | Lying in the garden | At work on a farm | At the doctor's shop
test: Which phrase is the respectful way to talk about autism? | On the spectrum | Lost his marbles | Fond of a drink | Laid up
exam: Identify the plain meaning of the expression "in a comfortable condition" as employed in a hospital bulletin. | Seriously ill but stable | Fully recovered and content | Resting upon soft furnishings | Ready for discharge
exam: It is given that a radiograph is reported to show "a shadow on the lung". Determine what is thereby suggested. | The possible presence of a tumour in the lung | A defect in the photographic apparatus | The presence of a person behind the patient | The excellent health of the lung
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the term "halitosis". | A medical term for bad breath | A colloquial term for a headache | A medical term for baldness | A colloquial term for hiccups
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression "self-medicating" when applied to a person's consumption of alcohol. | Drinking alcohol in order to cope with difficulties | Taking prescribed medicine correctly and at the stated times | Manufacturing one's own medicine | Treating others without qualification
